---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Crumbs from '../components/Crumbs.astro';
import SystemMap from '../components/SystemMap.astro';
import GiversChecklist from '../components/GiversChecklist.astro';
import { DEPTH, EVIDENCE, GRADE, UPSTREAM } from '../lib/labels';
import { url } from '../lib/site';
import frameworksData from '../data/frameworks.json';

const SECTIONS = [
  ['system', 'See the system'],
  ['depth', 'Measure depth'],
  ['evidence', 'Grade the evidence'],
  ['counterfactual', 'Ask what would happen anyway'],
  ['frameworks', 'Look through every lens'],
  ['time', 'Mind the horizon'],
  ['feedback', 'Close the loop'],
  ['checklist', 'The Giver’s Checklist'],
  ['honesty', 'The honesty rules'],
];
---

<Base
  title="The Charitology Method"
  description="Nine instruments a charitologist uses on any gift, program or system: from seeing the system and measuring depth to the Giver’s Checklist."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <Crumbs items={[{ label: 'Method' }]} />
      <span class="eyebrow">The Method</span>
      <h1>How a charitologist thinks</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Nine instruments you can use on any gift, program or system. None of them needs a degree. All of them get better
        with practice, which is what makes charitology a craft.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="wrap section-tight method">
    <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <ol>
        {
          SECTIONS.map(([id, label], i) => (
            <li>
              <a href={`#${id}`}>
                <span class="numeral">{i + 1}</span> {label}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <div class="method-body">
      <section id="system" class="m-section">
        <span class="eyebrow">Instrument 1</span>
        <h2>See the system</h2>
        <p>
          Start by placing any gift in the system it moves through: who gives, what signals they see, which channel holds
          the money, what work it pays for, what changes, and whether anyone finds out. Most problems in giving aren’t
          about one gift. They’re about the system’s habits. The <a href={url('/diagnosis/')}>Diagnosis</a> names fifteen of
          them.
        </p>
        <SystemMap compact />
      </section>

      <section id="depth" class="m-section">
        <span class="eyebrow">Instrument 2</span>
        <h2>Measure depth</h2>
        <p>
          Every piece of work acts somewhere on the chain from <a href={url('/record/encyclopedia/symptoms-and-sources/')}
            >symptom to source</a
          >. Charitology uses five levels. Depth describes the work; it never grades it. Relief keeps people alive while
          sources are fixed, and every level is needed. The question is always the mix.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="data depth-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Level</th><th>What it does</th><th>Question to ask</th><th>Example</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {
                DEPTH.map((d) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>
                      <span class={`swatch d${d.level}`} aria-hidden="true" />
                      <strong>
                        {d.level} · {d.name}
                      </strong>
                    </td>
                    <td>{d.verb}</td>
                    <td>{d.question}</td>
                    <td>{d.example}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Level 4 takes its sources from <a href="https://humanityschecklist.org/">Humanity’s Checklist</a>, which names six
          roots upstream of every world problem:
          {
            Object.values(UPSTREAM)
              .map((u) => u.label.toLowerCase())
              .join(', ')
          }. Whether fixing them really helps everything downstream is a hypothesis charitology exists to test.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="evidence" class="m-section">
        <span class="eyebrow">Instrument 3</span>
        <h2>Grade the evidence</h2>
        <p>Every claim carries a label. There are two systems, one for claims about the charity system and one for interventions.</p>
        <div class="two">
          <div>
            <h3>Claims about the system</h3>
            <dl class="labels">
              {
                Object.entries(EVIDENCE).map(([, e]) => (
                  <>
                    <dt>
                      <span aria-hidden="true">{e.glyph}</span> {e.label}
                    </dt>
                    <dd>{e.note}</dd>
                  </>
                ))
              }
            </dl>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Interventions</h3>
            <dl class="labels">
              {
                Object.entries(GRADE).map(([, g]) => (
                  <>
                    <dt>{g.label}</dt>
                    <dd>{g.note}</dd>
                  </>
                ))
              }
            </dl>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>
          Look for outcomes, not activity: lives changed, not meals counted. See <a
            href={url('/record/encyclopedia/outputs-and-outcomes/')}>outputs and outcomes</a
          > and <a href={url('/diagnosis/measurement-gap/')}>the Measurement Gap</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="counterfactual" class="m-section">
        <span class="eyebrow">Instrument 4</span>
        <h2>Ask what would happen anyway</h2>
        <p>
          The real effect of a gift is the difference between the world with it and the world without it. If the work would
          be funded anyway, your gift’s effect lands somewhere else. That’s why <a
            href={url('/record/encyclopedia/neglectedness/')}>neglect</a
          > matters: an extra dollar does more where few dollars go. See <a
            href={url('/record/encyclopedia/counterfactual-impact/')}>counterfactual impact</a
          > and <a href={url('/open-problems/the-counterfactual-problem/')}>Open Problem 4</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="frameworks" class="m-section">
        <span class="eyebrow">Instrument 5</span>
        <h2>Look through every lens</h2>
        <p>
          Every moral framework makes some kinds of good easy to see and others invisible. Charitology looks through all of
          them, reports where they agree, and is honest where they don’t. These are the eight it uses today:
        </p>
        <div class="frames">
          {
            frameworksData.frameworks.map((f) => (
              <div class="frame">
                <h3>{f.name}</h3>
                <p class="frame-q">{f.question}</p>
                <p class="note">{f.thinkers.join(', ')}</p>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
        <p>
          Find your own mix with the <a href={url('/tools/values-compass/')}>Values Compass</a>, then turn it into a budget with
          the <a href={url('/tools/portfolio-builder/')}>Portfolio Builder</a>. Seeing through only one lens is <a
            href={url('/diagnosis/single-lens/')}>the Single Lens</a
          >.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="time" class="m-section">
        <span class="eyebrow">Instrument 6</span>
        <h2>Mind the horizon</h2>
        <p>
          Ask how long the good lasts and who it reaches across time. Some good ends when the money stops. Some compounds for
          generations. Charity runs on annual budgets while its deepest problems run on decades, which is <a
            href={url('/diagnosis/short-horizon/')}>the Short Horizon</a
          >. How much the future should count is <a href={url('/open-problems/discounting-the-future/')}>Open Problem 13</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="feedback" class="m-section">
        <span class="eyebrow">Instrument 7</span>
        <h2>Close the loop</h2>
        <p>
          A donor may be paying for work whose results they cannot observe directly. Ask how anyone would find out if the work stopped working, and
          whether the people it serves get a say. See <a href={url('/diagnosis/missing-feedback-loop/')}
            >the Missing Feedback Loop</a
          > and <a href={url('/diagnosis/missing-voice/')}>the Missing Voice</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="checklist" class="m-section">
        <span class="eyebrow">Instrument 8</span>
        <h2>The Giver’s Checklist</h2>
        <p>
          Ten questions to ask of any gift, large or small. Tick them off as you go. Nothing is saved or sent. The <a
            href={url('/tools/browser-extension/')}>browser extension</a
          > puts the same list on donation pages.
        </p>
        <GiversChecklist />
      </section>

      <section id="honesty" class="m-section">
        <span class="eyebrow">Instrument 9</span>
        <h2>The honesty rules</h2>
        <ol class="rules">
          <li><strong>Unknown stays unknown.</strong> Say what has not been measured here, without assuming nobody else has studied it.</li>
          <li>
            <strong>Charitology’s own claims are hypotheses</strong> until tested, and the Diagnosis labels them that way.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No ratings of named charities</strong> until the method has passed public <a href={url('/programs/red-team/')}
              >red-team</a
            > review. Depth classifications are descriptions, open to challenge.
          </li>
          <li><strong>Every claim has a source</strong>, and every page links to a public correction.</li>
          <li><strong>Failures are published.</strong> Including Charitology’s own.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>The full standard is in <a href={url('/record/methodology/')}>the Methodology Handbook</a>.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .method {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2rem 3.5rem;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }
  @media (min-width: 980px) {
    .method {
      grid-template-columns: 240px minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
    .toc {
      position: sticky;
      top: calc(var(--header-h) + 24px);
      align-self: start;
    }
  }
  .toc ol {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.15rem;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  .toc a {
    display: block;
    padding: 0.4rem 0.6rem;
    border-radius: 7px;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .toc a:hover {
    background: var(--bg-2);
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .toc .numeral {
    display: inline-block;
    width: 1.4rem;
  }
  .method-body {
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .m-section {
    padding-bottom: 2.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    max-width: 920px;
  }
  .m-section:last-child {
    border-bottom: 0;
  }
  .m-section > p {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .swatch {
    display: inline-block;
    width: 18px;
    height: 10px;
    border-radius: 2px;
    margin-right: 0.45rem;
  }
  .depth-table td {
    min-width: 140px;
  }
  .two {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.5rem 2.5rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 300px), 1fr));
    margin: 1rem 0 1.25rem;
  }
  .two h3 {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
  }
  .labels {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  .labels dt {
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
  }
  .labels dd {
    margin: 0.15rem 0 0;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .frames {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(min(100%, 210px), 1fr));
    margin: 1.25rem 0;
  }
  .frame {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    padding: 0.9rem 1rem;
  }
  .frame h3 {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.3rem;
  }
  .frame-q {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    line-height: 1.4;
  }
  .rules {
    padding-left: 1.3rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
</style>
